= a
{parent=M1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The sequence is a <Markov chain> when the conditional law of its next state depends on the past only through the current state. Precisely, for each history of positive <probability> and every $j\in S$,
$$
\boxed{\Pr(X_{n+1}=j\mid X_n=i_n,\ldots,X_0=i_0)
=\Pr(X_{n+1}=j\mid X_n=i_n).}
$$
A time-homogeneous chain additionally has transition probabilities independent of $n$, written $P_{ij}=\Pr(X_{n+1}=j\mid X_n=i)$. The displayed single transition matrix in the subsequent parts specifies this time-homogeneous case.

= b
{parent=M1}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

First the entries must be nonnegative: together with the given $a,b\geq0$, validity of the <transition matrix> requires $0\leq a\leq1$ and $0\leq b\leq1/3$. Every row then sums to one.

The only incoming edge to state $2$ is $1\to2$, of <probability> $a$. If $a=0$, no other state can reach $2$, so the chain is not irreducible. If $a>0$, all edges in the directed cycle
$$
1\longrightarrow2\longrightarrow3\longrightarrow4\longrightarrow1
$$
have positive <probability>, since $P_{23}=1/3+b>0$, $P_{34}=1$ and $P_{41}=1/2$. Traversing this cycle connects every state to every other state. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\text{Irreducible exactly when }0<a\leq1,\quad0\leq b\leq\frac13.}
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Within the valid parameter rectangle, suppose $a>0$ and $b<1/3$. The chain is then irreducible by part (i). State $1$ has a two-step return $1\to2\to1$ and a three-step return $1\to2\to4\to1$, both of positive <probability>. Its <period of a state in a Markov chain> therefore divides both two and three, hence equals one. Irreducibility gives the same period to every state, so the chain is aperiodic.

If $b=1/3$ and $a>0$, every transition goes between the two classes $\{1,3\}$ and $\{2,4\}$. Returns must have even length, while $1\to2\to1$ has length two. Thus the irreducible chain has period two.

If $a=0$, the closed communicating class $\{1,3,4\}$ alternates between $\{1,3\}$ and $\{4\}$. Its states have period two, using the positive two-step returns through $4$. Consequently the chain is not aperiodic, regardless of how the transient state $2$ is classified. Combining the cases,
$$
\boxed{\text{Aperiodic exactly when }0<a\leq1,\quad0\leq b<\frac13.}
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Here the valid parameters lie in the irreducible aperiodic range. The finite-state theorem on <convergence to an invariant distribution> therefore implies convergence to the unique <stationary distribution>, independently of the initial state.

Solve $\pi P=\pi$ and $\sum_i\pi_i=1$. The equations for states $1,2,3$ give
$$
\pi_2=\frac12\pi_1,\qquad
\pi_1=\frac13\pi_2+\frac12\pi_4,\qquad
\pi_3=\frac13\pi_2+\frac12\pi_4.
$$
Hence $\pi_3=\pi_1$ and $\pi_4=5\pi_1/3$. Normalization gives $\pi_1(1+1/2+1+5/3)=1$, so
$$
\pi=\frac1{25}(6,3,6,10),\qquad
\boxed{\lim_{n\to\infty}\Pr(X_n=1\mid X_0=2)=\frac6{25}.}
$$
Stationarity alone would not guarantee convergence of the one-time distributions; irreducibility and aperiodicity justify that final limit.

= M2
{parent=M}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=M2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A <joint probability density function> must be measurable, nonnegative almost everywhere, and normalized:
$$
\boxed{f(x,y)\geq0\text{ almost everywhere},\qquad
\int_{\mathbb R^2}f(x,y)\,dx\,dy=1.}
$$
It determines the joint law by $\Pr((X,Y)\in B)=\int_B f(x,y)\,dx\,dy$ for measurable sets $B$. Values on sets of planar measure zero may be changed without changing that law.

= b
{parent=M2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

**The variables are not independent.** Their joint support excludes $X>1/2$ and $Y>1/2$ simultaneously, since every point with positive joint density satisfies $X+Y\leq1$. Yet each event separately has positive <probability>: the density is strictly positive on open triangular subregions where $X>1/2$, and similarly where $Y>1/2$. Thus
$$
\Pr(X>1/2,Y>1/2)=0<\Pr(X>1/2)\Pr(Y>1/2).
$$
This contradicts the event factorization required for <independence of random variables>. The product-shaped formula $24xy$ on its support does not undo the dependence created by the triangular support.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For $0\leq x\leq1$, integrate the <joint probability density function> over its allowed $y$-range:
$$
f_X(x)=\int_0^{1-x}24xy\,dy=12x(1-x)^2.
$$
Outside this interval it is zero. Therefore the <marginal density> is
$$
\boxed{f_X(x)=\begin{cases}12x(1-x)^2,&0\leq x\leq1,\\0,&\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}}
$$
Its integral is $12(1/2-2/3+1/4)=1$, also checking the original joint normalization. By symmetry $Y$ has the same marginal density.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

From the marginal density,
$$
\mathbb E X=12\int_0^1x^2(1-x)^2\,dx=\frac25,
\qquad\mathbb E Y=\frac25.
$$
The mixed moment is
$$
\mathbb E[XY]=\int_0^1\int_0^{1-x}24x^2y^2\,dy\,dx
=8\int_0^1x^2(1-x)^3\,dx=\frac2{15}.
$$
Thus the <covariance> is
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)=\frac2{15}-\frac4{25}=-\frac2{75}.}
$$
The negative sign is consistent with the support constraint: a large value of one variable leaves a smaller available range for the other.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

For $0<x<1$, the conditional density is obtained by dividing the joint density by the positive marginal density:
$$
f_{Y\mid X=x}(y)=\frac{24xy}{12x(1-x)^2}
=\frac{2y}{(1-x)^2},\qquad0\leq y\leq1-x.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{f_{Y\mid X=1/2}(y)=\begin{cases}8y,&0\leq y\leq1/2,\\0,&\text{otherwise},\end{cases}}
$$
which integrates to one. Its <conditional expectation> is
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E(Y\mid X=1/2)=\int_0^{1/2}y(8y)\,dy=\frac13.}
$$
This uses the usual continuous conditional-density version, defined by the density ratio at a point where $f_X$ is positive; conditioning does not require the event $X=1/2$ itself to have positive <probability>.

= O
{parent=2012}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Statistics}

= O1
{parent=O}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=O1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Let $\ell_n(\theta)=\sum_{i=1}^n\log f(X_i;\theta)$ be the <log-likelihood>. Under the usual regularity conditions, the expected <Fisher information> in the whole sample is
$$
\boxed{I_n(\theta)=\mathbb E_\theta\!\left[\{\ell_n'(\theta)\}^2\right]
=-\mathbb E_\theta[\ell_n''(\theta)]=nI_1(\theta).}
$$
The equality of the two expressions uses <mean>-zero score and permitted differentiation of the normalization identity. Independent observations make the information additive. The <maximum-likelihood estimator> has <asymptotic normality>:
$$
\sqrt n(\widehat\theta-\theta)\xrightarrow{d}N(0,I_1(\theta)^{-1}),
\qquad
\boxed{\widehat\theta\ \text{approximately}\ N(\theta,I_n(\theta)^{-1}).}
$$
The factor $n$ is included in $I_n$; it must not be inserted a second time into the <variance>.

= b
{parent=O1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $z=\Phi^{-1}(1-\alpha/2)$ for the standard normal cumulative distribution function. Consistency of the <maximum-likelihood estimator> and <Slutsky's theorem> give the asymptotic standardization
$$
\sqrt{I_n(\widehat\theta)}(\widehat\theta-\theta)\xrightarrow{d}N(0,1).
$$
Its <probability> of lying between $-z$ and $z$ tends to $1-\alpha$. Solving those inequalities for the unknown parameter yields the <Wald confidence interval>
$$
\boxed{\left[\widehat\theta-\frac{z}{\sqrt{I_n(\widehat\theta)}},\quad
\widehat\theta+\frac{z}{\sqrt{I_n(\widehat\theta)}}\right].}
$$
Thus the estimated <standard error> is $I_n(\widehat\theta)^{-1/2}$. Its coverage is approximate and relies on the large-sample regularity assumptions.

= c
{parent=O1}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For the stated <geometric distribution>, write $S=\sum_iX_i$. Then
$$
\ell_n(\theta)=n\log\theta+(S-n)\log(1-\theta),
\qquad
-\ell_n''(\theta)=\frac n{\theta^2}+\frac{S-n}{(1-\theta)^2}.
$$
Since $\mathbb E X_i=1/\theta$, we have $\mathbb E(S-n)=n(1-\theta)/\theta$. Taking <expectation> gives the sample <Fisher information>
$$
I_n(\theta)=\frac n{\theta^2}+\frac n{\theta(1-\theta)}
=\boxed{\frac n{\theta^2(1-\theta)}}.
$$
The support begins at one, so this <expectation> uses the trials-until-first-success convention of the geometric law.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For the <Bernoulli distribution>, with $S=\sum_iX_i$,
$$
\ell_n(\theta)=S\log\theta+(n-S)\log(1-\theta),
\qquad
-\ell_n''(\theta)=\frac S{\theta^2}+\frac{n-S}{(1-\theta)^2}.
$$
Using $\mathbb ES=n\theta$, the expected <Fisher information> is
$$
I_n(\theta)=\frac n\theta+\frac n{1-\theta}
=\boxed{\frac n{\theta(1-\theta)}}.
$$

= d
{parent=O1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For the same sample size $n$, the <geometric distribution> provides more <Fisher information>:
$$
\frac{I_n^{\rm geom}(\theta)}{I_n^{\rm Bern}(\theta)}=\frac1\theta>1
\qquad(0<\theta<1).
$$
The corresponding asymptotic variances of the <maximum-likelihood estimators> are
$$
\operatorname{Var}(\widehat\theta_{\rm geom})\approx\frac{\theta^2(1-\theta)}n,
\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(\widehat\theta_{\rm Bern})\approx\frac{\theta(1-\theta)}n.
$$
Hence **choose the geometric sample for more precise inference at equal numbers of observations**. Its asymptotic <variance> is $\theta$ times as large, and its <standard error> is $\sqrt\theta$ times as large. This illustrates <Fisher information per observation and per trial>. It is a per-observation comparison: one geometric observation records a waiting time containing, on average, $1/\theta$ Bernoulli trials. A constraint on the total number of underlying trials would be a different sampling-budget comparison.

= O2
{parent=O}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=O2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For the full-column-rank <design matrix>, the normal-error <maximum-likelihood estimator> is the least-squares estimator
$$
\boxed{\widehat\theta=(X^TX)^{-1}X^TY.}
$$
Indeed maximizing the Gaussian likelihood over $\theta$ minimizes $\|Y-X\theta\|^2$, whose <normal equations> are $X^TX\widehat\theta=X^TY$; rank $p$ makes $X^TX$ invertible. Substitution of the model gives
$$
\widehat\theta=\theta+(X^TX)^{-1}X^T\epsilon.
$$
Since $\mathbb E\epsilon=0$, it follows that $\boxed{\mathbb E\widehat\theta=\theta}$, proving unbiasedness.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Independence and common error <variance> give $\operatorname{Var}(\epsilon)=\sigma^2I_n$. For the fixed matrix $A=(X^TX)^{-1}X^T$, the <covariance> transformation rule gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Var}(\widehat\theta)&=A\operatorname{Var}(\epsilon)A^T\\
&=\sigma^2(X^TX)^{-1}X^TX(X^TX)^{-1}\\
&=\boxed{\sigma^2(X^TX)^{-1}}.
\end{aligned}
$$
Thus the <covariance> is determined by the error <variance> and the inverse <Gram matrix> of the design columns.

= b
{parent=O2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The design columns are $1$, $x$ and $z$. Their pairwise inner products are zero by the three supplied sums. Put $S_{xx}=\sum_ix_i^2$ and $S_{zz}=\sum_iz_i^2$, positive under the full-rank assumption. Then
$$
X^TX=\operatorname{diag}(n,S_{xx},S_{zz}).
$$
Applying part (a),
$$
\boxed{\widehat\theta=\begin{pmatrix}\widehat\alpha\\\widehat\beta\\\widehat\gamma\end{pmatrix}
=\begin{pmatrix}\overline Y\\\displaystyle\frac{\sum_ix_iY_i}{S_{xx}}\\\displaystyle\frac{\sum_iz_iY_i}{S_{zz}}\end{pmatrix},
\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(\widehat\theta)=\sigma^2\operatorname{diag}\left(\frac1n,\frac1{S_{xx}},\frac1{S_{zz}}\right).}
$$
The off-diagonal covariances vanish because the design columns are <orthogonal>. With Gaussian errors, the coefficient estimators are also jointly normal and therefore independent.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The future <mean> response is $E_*=\alpha+\beta x_*+\gamma z_*$. By invariance of <maximum likelihood estimation>, its estimator is the fitted <mean>
$$
\boxed{\widehat E_*=\widehat\alpha+x_*\widehat\beta+z_*\widehat\gamma.}
$$
Its sampling <variance> is the quadratic form of the coefficient <covariance matrix> with $c=(1,x_*,z_*)^T$:
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Var}(\widehat E_*)=c^T\operatorname{Var}(\widehat\theta)c
=\sigma^2\left(\frac1n+\frac{x_*^2}{S_{xx}}+\frac{z_*^2}{S_{zz}}\right).}
$$
This estimator is unbiased because each fitted coefficient is unbiased. The future error $\epsilon_*$ does not enter the estimator of the <mean>, so its independent <variance> is not part of this expression.

= P
{parent=2012}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Numerical Analysis}

= P1
{parent=P}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=P1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A polynomial $p_n\in\Pi_n$ is a best <polynomial least-squares approximation> when it minimizes the weighted error <norm> over all polynomials of degree at most $n$:
$$
\boxed{\|f-p_n\|\leq\|f-q\|\quad\text{for every }q\in\Pi_n.}
$$
Equivalently it minimizes $\int_0^1x^{-1/2}(f(x)-q(x))^2\,dx$. The weight is integrable, so the expression is finite for continuous functions; it defines a genuine <norm> because a continuous function with zero weighted squared integral must be identically zero.

= b
{parent=P1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Take any $q\in\Pi_n$. Write $f-q=(f-p_n)+(p_n-q)$. The second term belongs to $\Pi_n$, so the assumed orthogonality gives $\langle f-p_n,p_n-q\rangle=0$. Expanding the squared <norm> yields the <Pythagorean theorem> in this <inner product>:
$$
\|f-q\|^2=\|f-p_n\|^2+\|p_n-q\|^2\geq\|f-p_n\|^2.
$$
Taking square roots proves
$$
\boxed{p_n\text{ is a best least-squares approximation}.}
$$
Equality can hold only when $\|p_n-q\|=0$, hence $p_n=q$, so the best polynomial is unique. Completeness of the ambient space is unnecessary for this orthogonality argument.

= c
{parent=P1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Write $p_1(x)=a+bx$. It suffices to impose orthogonality of the error to the basis $1,x$ of $\Pi_1$. With the printed weight $x^{-1/2}$ and $f=x^{3/2}$, the <normal equations> are
$$
\begin{pmatrix}2&2/3\\2/3&2/5\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}a\\b\end{pmatrix}
=\begin{pmatrix}1/2\\1/3\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Here, for example, $\langle f,1\rangle=\int_0^1x\,dx=1/2$ and $\langle f,x\rangle=\int_0^1x^2\,dx=1/3$. Solving gives $a=-1/16$, $b=15/16$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{p_1(x)=\frac{15x-1}{16}.}
$$
The two <normal equations> make the error <orthogonal> to every linear polynomial, and part (b) then proves optimality, rather than merely furnishing a candidate fit.

= P2
{parent=P}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=P2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The leading error term can be removed by comparing the two step sizes $h$ and $h/2$:
$$
I_h=I-K_2h^2-K_4h^4-\cdots,\qquad
I_{h/2}=I-\frac14K_2h^2-\frac1{16}K_4h^4-\cdots.
$$
Multiply the second expression by four and subtract the first. The $h^2$ terms cancel, so the first <Richardson extrapolation> step is
$$
\boxed{R_1(h)=\frac{4I_{h/2}-I_h}{3},\qquad I-R_1(h)=-\frac14K_4h^4+O(h^6).}
$$
Repeat with $R_1(h/2)$ and $R_1(h)$, now cancelling the fourth-order error using the factor $16$. In general, with $R_0(h)=I_h$,
$$
\boxed{R_j(h)=\frac{4^jR_{j-1}(h/2)-R_{j-1}(h)}{4^j-1}.}
$$
Under the stated even-power expansion, successive columns have errors of orders $h^4,h^6,h^8,\ldots$. Each step uses the known scaling of the leading error to eliminate its unknown coefficient.

= b
{parent=P2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For one interval, the <trapezoidal rule> gives
$$
\boxed{I_1=\frac12\{0^4+1^4\}=\frac12.}
$$
For two intervals of width $h=1/2$, the composite <trapezoidal rule> gives half weights at the endpoints and full weight at the internal node:
$$
\boxed{I_{1/2}=\frac12\left\{\frac12\,0^4+\left(\frac12\right)^4+\frac12\,1^4\right\}
=\frac9{32}.}
$$

= c
{parent=P2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Apply the first <Richardson extrapolation> step to the two computed approximations:
$$
\boxed{R_1(1)=\frac{4(9/32)-1/2}{3}=\frac5{24}.}
$$
The exact integral is $I=\int_0^1x^4\,dx=1/5$, so the actual absolute error is
$$
\boxed{\left|\frac5{24}-\frac15\right|=\frac1{120}\approx0.00833<10^{-2}.}
$$
Thus the requested strict error bound is verified directly. This first extrapolation of trapezoidal approximations is also the one-panel <Simpson's rule> value on the three nodes $0,1/2,1$.
